# Entry point for a new session

This file is **state**, not architecture. Architecture and pitfalls live in `PROJECT.md` and change
rarely; here is everything that goes stale within a wave of work, and it is rewritten in full at the end of each wave,
not appended at the bottom.

Why: a session starting from the two words "continue" and a link here spends minutes on reconnaissance instead of
an hour. The measured case — a run where the order of work, mines with addresses in the code and reversed earlier
decisions lay in one place; without this every new session derives them anew and trips over the same thing a second time.

## Where we are now

- **Closed:** <what is finished and merged — briefly, without retelling the tasks>
- **Next:** <task numbers in order, with one line "why this order">
- **Do not touch:** <work someone else is doing right now>

## Mines found by READING the code

Only with addresses (`file:line`) and only what was verified by eye in the code. An assumption is not a mine:
it will cost the next session a pass and save none.

- `<path:line>` — <what is actually there and why it is a trap>

## Exhausted budgets

What the next task will run into if not known in advance: linter limits at the threshold, query counters
with no headroom, files at the size limit. Measured case: the same module-members limit
was hit three times in one session, and each time it was discovered AFTER the code was written.

- `<what>` — <how much is left to the threshold>

## Reversed decisions

Decisions that look in force but are reversed. Without this list the next session reproduces
them in good faith, by the document.

- <what was reversed> → <what instead> (<when and by whom decided>)
